rsion":"2.1.321","gitBranch":"jusprin-dev","slug":"rosy-bubbling-whisper"}
{"parentUuid":"c83a3b06-605e-4b33-83fc-584c4f2602fa","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"e9204a25-2670-4a9c-a6c1-5f0039de6045","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012GSx3Gkei9TdDQPLog1dGg","type":"tool_result","content":"   ingress's static IP, the one `app-stg.obico.io` and `app.obico.io` resolve\n   to.\n2. `kubectl apply -f tsd/gcp/jusprin-managed-cert.yaml`, then\n   `kubectl apply -f tsd/gcp/neg-ingress.yaml` (the ingress names the\n   certificate, so the certificate goes first). A certificate is issued once\n   its name resolves to the ingress. Both hosts are in the files:\n   `gke-managed-cert-jusprinv2-app` serves `jusprinv2.obico.io`, and its rule\n   routes like `app.obico.io`'s.\n3. On the server, first move the syndicate id sequence past the existing\n   rows: base and kingroon were inserted with explicit ids, so on 2026-11-04\n   staging's next id was 2 (kingroon's) and production's 1 (base's). In\n   `python manage.py shell` on a web pod:\n   `from django.core.management.color import no_style; from django.db import connection; from app.models import Syndicate; [connection.cursor().execute(s) for s in connection.ops.sequence_reset_sql(no_style(), [Syndicate])]`\n   (done on staging 2026-11-04 and production 2026-11-10). Without it the\n   next step fails and rolls back. Then:\n   `run_django_mgmt_cmd.sh set_up_jusprinv2_syndicate jusprinv2-stg.obico.io --google-from app-stg.obico.io`\n   (production: `jusprinv2.obico.io --google-from app.obico.io`). It makes the\n   `jusprinv2` syndicate and the site for the host, takes the site out of the\n   base syndicate that a new site joins automatically, and puts Obico's Google\n   sign-in app on it. Safe to run again.\n4. Google Cloud console, the OAuth client of that Google sign-in app: add\n   `https://<host>/accounts/google/login/callback/` as a redirect URI.\n5. The desktop app's default gateway (`ProviderProfile.hpp` in JusPrin, the\n   \"jusprin\" row) is `https://jusprinv2.obico.io/ent/jusprin/v2/responses`. To\n   test against staging, set `jusprin_server/base_url` to\n   `https://jusprinv2-stg.obico.io` in the app's settings file.\n\nJusPrin v1 users signed in with Obico accounts on `app.obico.io`. The v1 import\nleaves that sign-in behind, they sign up again on JusPrin's host, and their\nv1 subscriptions are moved to the new accounts by hand\n(`jusprin_subscription_status` lists both accounts of an address).","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"c0710f87-cc44-4bc9-a1cb-a0a4d328c0b6","timestamp":"2026-11-11T01:13:57.777Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"   ingress's static IP, the one `app-stg.obico.io` and `app.obico.io` resolve\n   to.\n2. `kubectl apply -f tsd/gcp/jusprin-managed-cert.yaml`, then\n   `kubectl apply -f tsd/gcp/neg-ingress.yaml` (the ingress names the\n   certificate, so the certificate goes first). A certificate is issued once\n   its name resolves to the ingress. Both hosts are in the files:\n   `gke-managed-cert-jusprinv2-app` serves `jusprinv2.obico.io`, and its rule\n   routes like `app.obico.io`'s.\n3. On the server, first move the syndicate id sequence past the existing\n   rows: base and kingroon were inserted with explicit ids, so on 2026-11-04\n   staging's next id was 2 (kingroon's) and production's 1 (base's). In\n   `python manage.py shell` on a web pod:\n   `from django.core.management.color import no_style; from django.db import connection; from app.models import Syndicate; [connection.cursor().execute(s) for s in connection.ops.sequence_reset_sql(no_style(), [Syndicate])]`\n   (done on staging 2026-11-04 and production 2026-11-10). Without it the\n   next step fails and rolls back. Then:\n   `run_django_mgmt_cmd.sh set_up_jusprinv2_syndicate jusprinv2-stg.obico.io --google-from app-stg.obico.io`\n   (production: `jusprinv2.obico.io --google-from app.obico.io`). It makes the\n   `jusprinv2` syndicate and the site for the host, takes the site out of the\n   base syndicate that a new site joins automatically, and puts Obico's Google\n   sign-in app on it. Safe to run again.\n4. Google Cloud console, the OAuth client of that Google sign-in app: add\n   `https://<host>/accounts/google/login/callback/` as a redirect URI.\n5. The desktop app's default gateway (`ProviderProfile.hpp` in JusPrin, the\n   \"jusprin\" row) is `https://jusprinv2.obico.io/ent/jusprin/v2/responses`. To\n   test against staging, set `jusprin_server/base_url` to\n   `https://jusprinv2-stg.obico.io` in the app's settings file.\n\nJusPrin v1 users signed in with Obico accounts on `app.obico.io`. The v1 import\nleaves that sign-in behind, they sign up again on JusPrin's host, and their\nv1 subscriptions are moved to the new accounts by hand\n(`jusprin_subscription_status` lists both accounts of an address).","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sandboxed":false,"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"c83a3b06-605e-4b33-83fc-584c4f2602fa","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/kenneth/Projects/ops","sessionId":"14cb6590-7113-489e-aa7f-9aa6dca15e79","version":"2.1.321","gitBranch":"jusprin-dev","slug":"rosy-bubbling-whisper"}
